WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.440
Every year Serbia loses a whole city. Unfortunately, it's not clickbait.

2
00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:20.950
In 2025 slightly fewer than 60,000 babies were born, which sounds like a solid number, but in the same period as many as 95,000 people died, so the population shrank by the size of one Pirot or Zaječar.

3
00:00:20.950 --> 00:00:28.860
For comparison, in 2015 65,000 babies were born, in 2005 72,000, while in 1950 as many as 163,000 babies were born.

4
00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:37.800
In fact, the last time that number was this low was in the 1870s, and that was when we had four times fewer inhabitants.

5
00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:47.820
However, that's not the end of the bad news. Between 2013 and 2024 almost half a million residents moved from Serbia into the European Union alone.

6
00:00:47.820 --> 00:01:02.010
The mass emigration of Serbs, not only toward the EU but to the rest of the world, additionally contributed to the population of our country shrinking from almost 8 million inhabitants in 1991 to only 6.5 million in mid-2025.

7
00:01:02.010 --> 00:01:10.560
If we go on like this, by 2052 we could lose another whole Belgrade and fall to only 5.2 million inhabitants.

8
00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:19.220
This data is given without Kosovo and Metohija, a region that is absolutely part of Serbia, but because of the hindered work of our institutions there we have no precise numbers.

9
00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:27.750
So what's going on, my dear temets? Why is Serbia disappearing? What are really all the causes and is there salvation for us, or will we really all have to fit under one plum tree?

10
00:01:27.750 --> 00:01:34.000
As always, to get to potential solutions, we first have to objectively look at why all this is happening to us.

11
00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:39.140
And don't worry, you're in the right place. Subscribe at least so this channel grows, because we're heading hard toward a million.

12
00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:46.740
Serbia is in the so-called demographic winter, a long-term period of population decline.

13
00:01:46.740 --> 00:01:50.460
Mathematically speaking, it's the result of two factors.

14
00:01:50.460 --> 00:01:54.570
The first is too few babies born compared to the number of deaths.

15
00:01:54.570 --> 00:02:01.240
That is, in expert terms, natural increase is negative because the birth rate is lower than the mortality rate.

16
00:02:01.400 --> 00:02:06.050
But there's a second factor too: more people leave Serbia than return,

17
00:02:06.050 --> 00:02:10.560
and the average age of a person who leaves the country is only 29.

18
00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:18.500
All this leads to the average resident of Serbia being as much as 44 years old, while almost a quarter of people are older than 65.

19
00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:22.240
And in this we are at the very top of the world, which is not good at all.

20
00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:25.220
So let's start with the first problem, too few babies born.

21
00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:28.280
We've already seen how dramatically the number of newborns has dropped.

22
00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:35.160
In theory, every woman needs to give birth to 2.1 children for the population to renew itself naturally from generation to generation.

23
00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:38.030
That is, of course, the so-called fertility rate,

24
00:02:38.030 --> 00:02:41.590
that is, not counting emigration and immigration into the country.

25
00:02:41.590 --> 00:02:50.020
In Serbia the fertility rate is only 1.64, and if we look back, we see that this number has been below that key 2.1 since the end of the 1950s.

26
00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:52.280
So it's not a new problem.

27
00:02:52.280 --> 00:02:58.130
It's interesting that at the beginning of the last century the fertility rate was as much as five births per woman.

28
00:02:58.130 --> 00:03:06.080
Now, the current 1.64 is actually slightly better than in more developed countries like the USA, where the average is only 1.34.

29
00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:14.410
And especially than Japan, China and South Korea, which are in literally a demographic collapse. In South Korea that rate has been below 1 for 9 years now,

30
00:03:14.410 --> 00:03:20.420
which brings us to a very awkward question: why do people have fewer children today than before?

31
00:03:20.600 --> 00:03:31.590
Well, there's no simple answer to this question. Older generations will tell you the young are lazy, Instagram users will tell you men are like this and women like that, but the truth is far more complicated than that.

32
00:03:31.590 --> 00:03:36.440
Low birth rate isn't only a consequence of our romantic relationship with our own smart devices.

33
00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:40.870
The epidemic of loneliness is truly alarming and definitely requires a separate video,

34
00:03:40.870 --> 00:03:44.450
but in Serbia a woman has on average fewer than two children for 70 years already,

35
00:03:44.450 --> 00:03:48.130
while people have been leaving the country in large numbers since the end of the Second World War,

36
00:03:48.130 --> 00:03:52.240
which means we're talking about a long-term problem, and in that we're not unique at all.

37
00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:59.130
The global fertility rate in 1963 was about 5.3 children per woman, while today it's only about 2.2.

38
00:03:59.130 --> 00:04:00.780
So what is going on here?

39
00:04:00.780 --> 00:04:04.680
Look, everything is different today than it was 60 and 70 years ago.

40
00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:09.740
First, women's rights have improved drastically compared to the 19th and first half of the 20th century.

41
00:04:09.740 --> 00:04:16.080
Because of that, women fought for the possibility to study, get employed and decide on their own future independently.

42
00:04:16.080 --> 00:04:23.700
For example, before the First World War women made up only 10% of students at the University of Belgrade. Today there are more of them than men.

43
00:04:23.700 --> 00:04:27.770
With studying and building a career, starting a family is logically postponed.

44
00:04:27.770 --> 00:04:35.340
In addition, reliable types of contraception developed, which gives a couple much greater control over when and how many children they'll have.

45
00:04:35.340 --> 00:04:43.540
So, one part of the historical fall in fertility is actually a consequence of huge civilizational progress, especially regarding women's rights and freedoms.

46
00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:49.240
Which is of course a positive thing, but demographers and economists found a direct correlation between those two things.

47
00:04:49.240 --> 00:04:54.800
Still, that's only one factor which can't explain everything. Today there are many more reasons and we'll get to them.

48
00:04:54.800 --> 00:04:58.000
Next, another big change is the drop in child mortality.

49
00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:03.880
For most of human history approximately half of the children born didn't live to see their fifth year.

50
00:05:03.880 --> 00:05:10.880
Because of such uncertainty, parents often had more children than they expected would actually reach adulthood.

51
00:05:10.880 --> 00:05:18.170
Progress in medicine, vaccination, hygiene, nutrition and public health dramatically increased the likelihood of children surviving.

52
00:05:18.170 --> 00:05:22.680
And, for example, today in Serbia that number has dropped to only five per thousand children.

53
00:05:22.680 --> 00:05:26.380
Another key factor is that children are no longer a labour force.

54
00:05:26.380 --> 00:05:27.690
Unfortunately, I'm not joking.

55
00:05:27.690 --> 00:05:33.120
In agrarian societies children were often employed from a young age, even in factories.

56
00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:38.370
Fortunately, industrialization, urbanization and the development of education gradually changed that.

57
00:05:38.370 --> 00:05:46.480
People realized that it's maybe not the smartest decision to drag a 6-year-old under heavy machinery after a 12-hour shift.

58
00:05:46.620 --> 00:05:48.840
I don't know how they came to that conclusion.

59
00:05:48.840 --> 00:05:52.660
Over time stricter regulations and compulsory schooling for children were introduced,

60
00:05:52.660 --> 00:05:57.850
which strictly economically speaking means that those children were no longer profitable. I know how that sounds.

61
00:05:57.850 --> 00:06:04.580
Because they stayed economically dependent on their parents for longer, while the costs of their upkeep and education grew.

62
00:06:04.580 --> 00:06:11.020
Parents therefore started to have fewer and fewer children, but to invest more resources into the education and development of each child.

63
00:06:11.140 --> 00:06:15.760
There's a mountain of reasons here, now we'll continue with those that concern us today.

64
00:06:15.760 --> 00:06:33.260
Today the most common problems are around reconciling career and family, and then of course instability on the labour market, general economic and social insecurity, dissatisfaction with social circumstances, the state of the country, ever longer schooling and of course big cultural changes compared to earlier generations.

65
00:06:33.260 --> 00:06:41.320
The result of all that is that parenthood has shifted much later, which means families have less and less time to have another child.

66
00:06:41.320 --> 00:06:49.240
For example, in 2024 the average age at which a mother had her first child was 29, while in 1950 it was under 23.

67
00:06:49.240 --> 00:06:57.290
This shift in when people decide on a first child automatically reduces the time in which a family can have a second or third child.

68
00:06:57.290 --> 00:07:02.530
So in the same period the fertility rate in Serbia halved, from about 3.5 to 1.6.

69
00:07:02.530 --> 00:07:11.800
And then when all that repeats over a couple of generations, you have fewer and fewer children, which means you have fewer and fewer future parents who will have even fewer children.

70
00:07:11.960 --> 00:07:18.490
So you fall into that vicious circle and then the age pyramid slowly but surely starts to stand upside down.

71
00:07:18.490 --> 00:07:29.640
And that means that even if the behaviour of the next generation changes suddenly and fertility rises five times, there will still be fewer of them than of us, which means they start from a smaller demographic base.

72
00:07:29.640 --> 00:07:33.440
So, for any significant change a lot of time is needed.

73
00:07:33.440 --> 00:07:36.280
Well, okay, temet, but I mean, what's so bad about it?

74
00:07:36.280 --> 00:07:40.270
Look, the point of this video isn't for me to lecture you about the importance of family.

75
00:07:40.270 --> 00:07:45.080
Besides, temet is a failed economist and will coldly stick only to the consequences for GDP.

76
00:07:45.300 --> 00:07:50.690
The number of primary school pupils shrank from about 710,000 to only 500,000 between 2000 and 2025.

77
00:07:50.690 --> 00:07:57.850
If fewer children are born, ten years later you have fewer pupils, and then another 15 years later you have fewer workers.

78
00:07:57.850 --> 00:08:01.910
Those workers finance the pension, health and social system,

79
00:08:01.910 --> 00:08:09.770
which means the younger generations, who will be fewer and fewer, will have to support old people, who will be more and more numerous.

80
00:08:09.770 --> 00:08:12.500
Which is an extraordinary burden for you and the whole system.

81
00:08:12.720 --> 00:08:18.280
So we will either retire later and later, or pay higher and higher taxes, or import workers from other countries.

82
00:08:18.280 --> 00:08:24.700
These are all of course issues and problems that are always accompanied by cultural debates in the comments section. Especially the last one.

83
00:08:24.840 --> 00:08:28.110
Come on temet, these young people are just lazy and don't want children.

84
00:08:28.110 --> 00:08:35.040
No, that's not true. Here's an example: in Germany young people aged 18 to 29 overwhelmingly want children, over 90%.

85
00:08:35.040 --> 00:08:39.350
In Serbia almost 80% of respondents would like to have at least two children.

86
00:08:39.350 --> 00:08:44.130
Well okay, when all these many people want children, what stops them?

87
00:08:44.130 --> 00:08:49.880
Well, people don't make decisions only on the basis of desire. The first thing that comes to mind is finances.

88
00:08:49.880 --> 00:08:57.460
Do you have a stable job, will you have to live for life in a windowless studio for 900 euros, or will you commit to the slavery of a loan,

89
00:08:57.460 --> 00:09:00.960
as well as how hard it will be to reconcile work with family life.

90
00:09:01.080 --> 00:09:13.280
If we stick to the money, in the second quarter of 2026 the average net salary was about 120,000 dinars, but it's more realistic to look at the median salary, which was about 94,000 "kinta", that is, 800 euros.

91
00:09:13.280 --> 00:09:16.980
That means half of the employed receive less than that amount.

92
00:09:16.980 --> 00:09:23.690
Why is all this important? Because at the beginning of 2026 the average consumer basket was about 920 euros.

93
00:09:23.690 --> 00:09:38.820
What is a consumer basket, for God's sake, my dear temets? It's a measure of how much an average family of three spends monthly on food, drinks, household upkeep, bills, fuel, clothes, going out and such nonsense, but without rent.

94
00:09:38.820 --> 00:09:43.410
Now, I assume you don't live under a rock, you know what the rents are like.

95
00:09:43.410 --> 00:09:49.570
It's quite realistic to say that in Belgrade another 600 to 700 euros go on rent. Let's say 650.

96
00:09:49.570 --> 00:09:53.540
The average consumer basket in Belgrade was about 970 euros.

97
00:09:53.540 --> 00:09:56.140
So, 970 plus 650 equals 1620 euros.

98
00:09:56.400 --> 00:10:01.180
Ugh, yes. In the same month the average salary in Belgrade was about 1230 euros.

99
00:10:01.180 --> 00:10:07.650
Although we don't have precise data, we can guess that the median salary in Belgrade was about 1000 euros.

100
00:10:07.650 --> 00:10:12.040
In case maths isn't your strong suit, 1000 is less than 1620.

101
00:10:12.040 --> 00:10:16.810
That means both parents have to work to scrape together 2000 euros together.

102
00:10:16.810 --> 00:10:20.670
And so together they're left with about 400 euros a month in their pocket,

103
00:10:20.670 --> 00:10:22.700
that is, about 4500 euros a year.

104
00:10:22.960 --> 00:10:26.010
That is, about 1.63 square metres, and that on Karaburma.

105
00:10:26.010 --> 00:10:31.310
With all respect to my home area, this is no joke of a negative spirit.

106
00:10:31.310 --> 00:10:40.190
I just want to stress that by this calculation it would take you and your partner about 37 years to buy a 60-square-metre flat in Karaburma.

107
00:10:40.190 --> 00:10:43.700
We'll fix the love for Burma when I come to power.

108
00:10:43.880 --> 00:10:47.090
So everyone has to work all day.

109
00:10:47.090 --> 00:10:51.560
But what if they try to scrape together that money abroad?

110
00:12:04.420 --> 00:12:06.320
So, back to the numbers.

111
00:12:06.320 --> 00:12:14.540
At the beginning of the video we established that within only 11 years about half a million people left for EU countries.

112
00:12:14.540 --> 00:12:19.740
Of course, people also leave in large numbers for America, Canada, Australia and so on.

113
00:12:19.740 --> 00:12:27.120
Some of those people even come back; when everything is added up, we're probably at a minus of about 15,000 people a year.

114
00:12:27.120 --> 00:12:29.440
It's impossible to establish that precisely,

115
00:12:29.440 --> 00:12:34.640
but as many as 900,000 people who were born in Serbia currently live outside the country.

116
00:12:34.820 --> 00:12:36.990
Still, the problem isn't only in that number.

117
00:12:36.990 --> 00:12:40.050
The average resident leaves Serbia at only 29.

118
00:12:40.050 --> 00:12:48.450
These are people who have just entered the period of life in which they should make up the biggest part of the workforce, then develop careers and start families.

119
00:12:48.450 --> 00:12:50.510
Their departure therefore hurts twice.

120
00:12:50.510 --> 00:12:54.850
Under A, the state immediately loses a able-bodied member of society at the peak of his strength.

121
00:12:54.850 --> 00:12:59.320
And under B, it loses his future family, which he'll perhaps start abroad.

122
00:12:59.460 --> 00:13:07.060
Today almost half of young people are thinking about leaving Serbia, while another 42% of them don't rule out that possibility.

123
00:13:07.060 --> 00:13:13.730
Yes, you heard right. Almost 90% of young people either think about or don't rule out the possibility of leaving the country.

124
00:13:13.730 --> 00:13:18.600
That's devastating, but it's not enough to say that it's all only because of money.

125
00:13:18.600 --> 00:13:25.320
Actually, when young people were asked why they're thinking about leaving the country, a third stated that they want a higher standard of living.

126
00:13:25.320 --> 00:13:30.770
But right after that came the wish for a more dignified life, then the wish for better public services,

127
00:13:30.770 --> 00:13:37.380
then more freedom and a better environment for starting a family, while a bigger chance of employment was stated by 5% of respondents.

128
00:13:37.600 --> 00:13:39.710
And here you have to understand something.

129
00:13:39.710 --> 00:13:43.930
Serbia is currently the fourth most corrupt country in Europe,

130
00:13:43.930 --> 00:13:48.920
while by the level of civil liberties and political rights it's only 109th in the world.

131
00:13:48.920 --> 00:13:53.700
The pollution is catastrophic, a canopy can fall on your head,

132
00:13:53.840 --> 00:14:01.640
we treat children with SMS messages, and on TV we watch people who poison this country and divert your attention from serious topics.

133
00:14:01.900 --> 00:14:05.220
A huge part of the population is still enchanted by a cult of personality

134
00:14:05.220 --> 00:14:12.160
and doesn't understand how those people betrayed their own country in a million different ways, but at least, you know, they built motorways.

135
00:14:17.240 --> 00:14:28.000
It should be stressed that this video was written and filmed before the students' victory, who will now have an absurd amount of work, from eradicating corruption to cleaning institutions and media of party cadres.

136
00:14:28.120 --> 00:14:33.380
Second, even if we talk about my favourite abbreviation, our GDP hasn't exploded to Singapore's level.

137
00:14:33.380 --> 00:14:39.880
With a nominal 17 thousand per capita, that is, 34 thousand by purchasing power parity, we're still at the tail of Europe.

138
00:14:39.880 --> 00:14:51.790
Maybe it's unfair to compare ourselves with Czechia and Poland, because they didn't have wars and sanctions, but Bulgaria, Romania and Serbia were at the same level in the 2000s, while today Bulgaria and Romania by this measure are already solidly ahead of us.

139
00:14:51.790 --> 00:14:54.310
The situation is similar with the development index.

140
00:14:54.310 --> 00:15:01.400
So corruption has been eating our potential for decades and, by the way, we already made a video on that a couple of years ago, take a look at it.

141
00:15:01.560 --> 00:15:03.820
Young people, thank God, are aware of this problem.

142
00:15:03.820 --> 00:15:13.970
Among them there is great dissatisfaction recorded with corruption, nepotism, political criteria in hiring, lack of justice and the insecurity of the legal, health and social protection systems.

143
00:15:13.970 --> 00:15:19.760
In other words, it's not only how much people earn that matters to them, but in what kind of system they're trying to build their lives.

144
00:15:19.960 --> 00:15:25.100
And here we come to a term that inevitably appears when we talk about emigration from Serbia.

145
00:15:25.100 --> 00:15:28.760
Brain drain. Temet with anglicisms again. Sorry. Odliv mozgova.

146
00:15:28.760 --> 00:15:39.560
Brain drain is the departure of highly qualified, highly educated people from a country that schooled them and invested money in them to some other country which got their expertise for free.

147
00:15:39.720 --> 00:15:48.420
In practice that usually means that qualified personnel from lower and medium developed countries go to highly developed countries in search of a better life.

148
00:15:48.420 --> 00:15:54.860
And those developed countries often do everything they can to attract qualified personnel from less developed countries.

149
00:15:54.860 --> 00:15:57.120
Now, this problem isn't uniquely Serbian.

150
00:15:57.120 --> 00:16:01.180
If we look at that brain drain index, Serbia is in 68th place in the world.

151
00:16:01.180 --> 00:16:03.500
Not great, not terrible, but pretty bad.

152
00:16:03.500 --> 00:16:08.020
What's tragic is that we're closer to countries at war than to developed countries.

153
00:16:08.160 --> 00:16:11.480
However, it's not only doctors of science and respected redditors who leave.

154
00:16:11.480 --> 00:16:18.510
Medical technicians, drivers, craftsmen, tradesmen and other categories of workers who are also key members of society leave too.

155
00:16:18.510 --> 00:16:20.820
So Serbia is damaged in all areas.

156
00:16:21.040 --> 00:16:25.430
Now, it's a bit tricky to quantify how much money Serbia actually loses this way,

157
00:16:25.430 --> 00:16:33.560
because the state invested money in the education of, say, an engineer, who then contributes with that knowledge to some other country he went to.

158
00:16:33.560 --> 00:16:39.300
Various estimates claim that Serbia loses up to 3 billion euros a year in GDP this way. Maybe, hard to say.

159
00:16:45.260 --> 00:16:52.440
On the other hand, the departure of our people doesn't depend only on problems in Serbia, but also on how other countries fish for our workers.

160
00:16:52.440 --> 00:16:58.980
Developed countries, for example Germany and Austria, also have negative natural increase and very low fertility.

161
00:16:58.980 --> 00:17:04.840
Consequently they have a great need for labour, which is why they actively try to attract foreign workers.

162
00:17:04.840 --> 00:17:08.120
The already existing Serbian diaspora also plays a particularly important role.

163
00:17:08.120 --> 00:17:13.740
Large communities of Serbs and generally other peoples represent significant support for new emigrants.

164
00:17:13.740 --> 00:17:21.730
In addition you have diaspora organizations, as well as programmes of foreign universities and institutions that constantly fish for talented students.

165
00:17:21.730 --> 00:17:28.100
Even around Belgrade itself you have ads to get a job abroad, which, when you think about it, is quite paradoxical.

166
00:17:28.100 --> 00:17:33.370
By the way, if you're wondering what's wrong with my voice, the first half of the video was recorded before my wedding, the second after.

167
00:17:33.370 --> 00:17:35.860
So sorry, but this video couldn't wait.

168
00:17:36.120 --> 00:17:40.720
And now you'll tell me: well okay, temet, but that diaspora sends money back.

169
00:17:40.720 --> 00:17:41.900
And you're right.

170
00:17:41.900 --> 00:17:50.970
Remittances, that is, money that people employed abroad send back to their families in Serbia, amount to about 5–8% of GDP, almost 5 billion euros a year.

171
00:17:50.970 --> 00:17:57.340
Besides money, the diaspora takes part in investments, donations and support for educational and cultural institutions,

172
00:17:57.340 --> 00:18:05.180
can contribute to the development of trade relations, connecting companies, as well as the international promotion of Serbia and the transfer of knowledge and technology.

173
00:18:05.180 --> 00:18:15.320
Yes, the diaspora is an important economic and social resource, but its positive sides cannot completely make up for the long-term loss of the young and able-bodied population.

174
00:18:33.340 --> 00:18:36.350
However, with this problem the solutions are a bit more complex.

175
00:18:36.350 --> 00:18:42.490
But given that most of the developed world is facing serious depopulation, we're not the only ones in this sauce.

176
00:18:42.490 --> 00:18:47.760
Some of the examples I'll present to you weren't overly successful, but we can learn something from them.

177
00:18:47.840 --> 00:18:51.760
Essentially, there are two ways to kick-start population growth.

178
00:18:51.760 --> 00:18:55.800
The first is to push people to have children, the second is to import workers.

179
00:18:55.800 --> 00:18:59.780
And the state tries both, but especially the second one causes a lot of controversy.

180
00:18:59.860 --> 00:19:07.180
As the number of domestic workers falls, Serbia tries to attract able-bodied people from abroad in a rush.

181
00:19:07.180 --> 00:19:13.170
Between 2014 and 2024 about 167,000 foreigners moved into Serbia, while about 72,000 moved out.

182
00:19:13.170 --> 00:19:17.430
And especially in the last couple of years the number of foreigners has grown considerably.

183
00:19:17.430 --> 00:19:21.690
Of course, the largest number are Russians, but that's a specific situation.

184
00:19:21.690 --> 00:19:29.150
In 2025 most visas for residence and work were actually issued to Chinese, with Indians, Bangladeshis and Nepalis right behind them.

185
00:19:29.150 --> 00:19:36.140
And not only that, the state is trying to speed up the issuing of work permits to foreigners, like the agreement with Uzbekistan.

186
00:19:36.220 --> 00:19:41.430
And then there's that famous story about 100,000 work permits for Ghanaians, or Ghanians, I have no idea,

